edition 5;

module mcp.tools;

// Generated by scripts/mcp.py from the tool's `introspect` and the compiler's
// authority report of the server -- do not edit (docs/mcp.md §3). The
// `tools/list` result, and for tool `i` the tables a call is checked against:
// its properties as flags (`property|flag|kind`, kind `bool`, `nat` or `text`),
// its operands in order (`property|min|max`, `max` empty for unbounded), how
// standard input reaches it (none, or `text`: a property called `stdin`), its output (`document` or `stream`), and the
// property that chooses its format (the answer is structured content only when
// it is `json`). Then the server's bounds and the sentences it says when it
// ends a call, and `introspect`.

pub fn list_result() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "{\"tools\":[{\"name\":\"table\",\"description\":\"A CSV or TSV file as a table, read in one bounded streaming pass over an RFC 4180 reader: its shape, some columns (--select), the rows that satisfy a condition (--where, bytewise or exact integers, decimals or doubles), rows in order (--order-by), or counts, sums, means, minima, maxima and distinct counts of groups (--group, --agg, --sort, --top), as json pages, as csv, or as JSON lines (--format jsonl: one object per row, keys the output columns, every value a string but the aggregates, which are numbers). Every refusal is a rule, with its hint. Four forms, chosen by the flags: the shape (FILE alone: the column names and the row count), rows (--select, --where or --order-by, then --top, --limit and --from to cut and page), groups (--group or --agg, then --where, --sort, --top, --limit and --from) and the type report (--report types, then --select, --where, --limit and --from: one row per column, saying what its cells are and which declaration would read it). A mix is refused. args.conflict: --select or --order-by with --group or --agg, --report with --group, --agg, --order-by, --sort or --top, and --format text with rows, groups or the report. The type report is not written as JSON lines (args.conflict). A column twice in the answer is output.duplicate-column for --format jsonl, and a column name that is not UTF-8 is output.not-representable. args.required-flag: --limit, --from or --format csv with the shape, --top with neither --order-by, --group nor --agg, and --sort without --group or --agg. --rows is every row with every column and no condition, and conflicts with --select, --where, --group, --agg, --order-by and --report. A join (--join FILE --on PROBE=BUILD) is a fifth form, rows only in this version: FILE or standard input is the probe side, streamed, and the --join file is the build side, read whole into memory before the first probe row and counted in --max-state-bytes by a stated formula (record bytes + 16 for each row held, key bytes + 60 for each distinct key), which is not resident memory: that can be about twice as much while the build grows. The answer is the inner join, as csv or json or JSON lines: for each probe row, in the probe's order, one row for each row of the --join file with an equal key, in that file's order, the probe row's cells and then the build row's cells (a probe row with no match is not in it, and fan-out is bounded by --max-join-rows). A key is compared as text, byte for byte: 007 is not 7, a quoted cell is the text inside its quotes, and an empty cell matches an empty cell (there is no null). A column name that both files have is join.column-collision, nothing is renamed on the way, unless --join-prefix gives the build file's columns a prefix; --select, --where, --limit, --from and --format work on the joined row. The build past --max-state-bytes is limit.state-too-large (detail.holder join-build), and one more joined row than --max-join-rows is limit.too-many-join-rows. A join reads on one core whatever --threads says, with the same bytes, and writes nothing. args.conflict also: --group, --agg, --order-by, --sort, --top or --report with --join (write the join as --format csv and pipe it into a second call), and --join with --input-format jsonl. --query asks one of the forms in a small subset of SQL (select, from, where, group by, order by, limit, offset) and is translated to the flags it stands for, so it gives the same bytes: it conflicts with --select, --where, --group, --agg, --sort, --order-by, --rows and --report, with --limit, --from or --top when the query has its own limit or offset, and with a FILE when it has a from. A query that is not in the grammar is refused as query.syntax at a byte offset with what was expected (and the corrected command when the correction is certain), what the language leaves out (or, like, null, an alias, an expression, a join) as query.unsupported with what to write instead, a select list that is not the group by columns then at least one aggregate as query.group-mismatch, and a query over 4096 bytes as query.too-long. The report reads every row once and changes nothing: its suggestions (:int, :dec(S), :float or none) are applied by no flag. --explain prints the plan that the other flags (or --query) resolve to, as one JSON line (data.explain: the source and its header, each condition, group, aggregate and order with its type, the output columns, the work and its bounds, the threads it would use and why, the rules the data could still refuse it with, notes), and does not run it: a file is opened and its first 64 KiB read for the header and nothing after it, standard input is not read, and a plan the run would refuse before it read a row is refused as the run refuses it. A page that was cut says data.next.from: pass it as --from. FILE is one path, relative to --root when given, and its first row is the header. With no FILE, or with - for FILE, the table is read from standard input in one pass on one core: the same answer as the file's, byte for byte (but that detail.path is -), whatever --threads says; a pipe is read in blocks on one core, so a large file is better named when --threads should help, and a read that fails is a refusal naming -. With --input-format jsonl (or ndjson) the input is JSON lines, one JSON object per line, and its columns are declared with --fields as paths (id, user.name, items.0.sku): every form above works on them unchanged, from a file or from standard input, with the same answers, the exact text of a number as the cell, a refusal for a line that is not strict JSON (parse.jsonl-syntax, with its offset), is not an object (parse.jsonl-not-object), has no value for a declared path (parse.jsonl-missing-field, or --absent empty), holds an object or an array there (parse.jsonl-not-scalar) or a key twice (parse.jsonl-duplicate-key), and --discover reports the paths and kinds the first lines hold when the columns are not known. The format is never guessed.\\n\\nThe file is read beneath the server's root: a path outside it, a `..` component or a link is refused with a rule (path.outside-root, path.dotdot, path.symlink); a table you hold as text goes in `stdin` instead of `file`. The answer is the tool's own output, byte for byte, and is bounded by the server: ask for fewer rows with `limit`, groups with `top`, columns with `select`. A refusal is an error result whose JSON names a `rule` and a `repair`; match on the rule.\",\"inputSchema\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"delimiter\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"the field separator: a comma, the word tab (or a tab character), or a semicolon\",\"default\":\",\"},\"select\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"the columns to return, a list: header names separated by commas, or #N for the Nth column (1-based), in the order given, and a repeat repeats the column. A comma, a backslash or a leading # in a name is written with a backslash before it. Examples: --select host,bytes and --select '#3,a\\\\,b' (the third column, then the column called a,b)\"},\"where\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"only the rows that satisfy this: conditions joined by and (no or, no parentheses), each COLUMN OP VALUE with OP one of = != < <= > >= contains, or COLUMN in (V1, V2). COLUMN is a header name or #N. With no suffix the cell is compared as text, bytewise, so 10 is less than 9. COLUMN:int compares exact integers, COLUMN:dec(N) exact decimals of at most N fractional digits, COLUMN:float doubles. A cell that is not one of those refuses the whole query (value.not-integer and the like), so keep such rows out first: bytes != '' and bytes:int > 5. A name or VALUE with spaces or operators is 'quoted' (a doubled quote is one quote). Examples: --where 'status = 404 and bytes:int > 1000' and --where 'host in (a, b) and path contains x'\"},\"group\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"count the rows by these columns instead of returning them: a list named as for --select, one row per distinct combination, in byte order unless --sort says otherwise. A name may end in :int, :dec(S) or :float to group by the value of the cell and not its text: 1.5 and 1.50 are one group, -0 is 0, the group is written at the column's scale (1.50) or as the shortest decimal that reads back (1.5), and the groups come in numeric order, negatives first. A cell that is not of that type refuses the query (value.not-decimal and the like, context group). A column read as one type must be read as it everywhere in the plan (column.type-conflict). A name that ends in :float is written name\\\\:float. Examples: --group host and --group host,status and --group 'price:dec(2),status'\"},\"agg\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"what to compute, a comma-separated list of items (default count). An item is count, or FUNCTION:COLUMN with FUNCTION one of sum, min, max, mean, distinct and COLUMN a header name or #N. With no suffix sum, min, max and mean read the cells as exact integers and distinct as text. A suffix on COLUMN says how to read it: :int, :dec(S) for exact decimals of at most S fractional digits (S is 0 to 18, never rounded) or :float for doubles (min, max, distinct, and the exact sum and mean: sum:x:float and mean:x:float give the exact sum, or the exact sum over the count, rounded once to the nearest double, the same for every --threads, and a sum beyond the largest double is agg.float-overflow. A float mean takes no @N, so mean:x:float@2 is agg.bad-spec). mean of integers or decimals is rounded half to even at @N fractional digits written after the column, as mean:bytes@2, and @N may be left out only for a :dec(S) column, whose scale is the default. The output column is the item without its suffixes, as sum:price. A column really called x:dec(2) is written sum:x\\\\:dec(2). With --group the answer has a row per group, alone one row for all the rows. Examples: --agg count,sum:bytes,max:bytes and --agg 'sum:price:dec(2),mean:bytes@1'\"},\"sort\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"with --group or --agg, order the groups by one output column: count, an aggregate as printed (sum:bytes, mean:price) or a group column, with - before it for descending. Aggregates compare as numbers and group columns bytewise (numerically for a typed --group column), ties go by the group columns. Without it the groups come in byte order of their columns. Examples: --sort -count and --sort sum:bytes\"},\"top\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"description\":\"keep only the first N rows or groups (N at least 1) after sorting: with --order-by the first N sorted rows, with --group or --agg the first N groups in --sort order, so it needs one of them. --limit and --from page inside it. Examples: --top 10 and --top 3\"},\"limit\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":1000000,\"description\":\"the most rows or groups in the answer (default 1000 as json, no limit as csv, ceiling 1000000), for --select, --where, --order-by, --group or --agg. A cut answer has truncated true and data.next.from, the --from of the next page. Examples: --limit 100 and --limit 20 --from 40\"},\"from\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"description\":\"the 0-based row or group to start at, as data.next.from of a truncated answer says, for --select, --where, --order-by, --group or --agg. Each call is a fresh read. Examples: --from 1000 and --from 2000\"},\"max-bytes\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":67108864,\"description\":\"the most json rows returned hold, past it the answer is truncated with a next (ceiling 67108864)\",\"default\":1048576},\"max-groups\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":1000000,\"description\":\"the most groups kept, one more is limit.too-many-groups (ceiling 1000000)\",\"default\":100000},\"max-distinct\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":10000000,\"description\":\"the most distinct values kept over all groups for distinct, one more is limit.too-many-distinct (ceiling 10000000)\",\"default\":100000},\"max-state-bytes\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":1073741824,\"description\":\"the most bytes of group keys, distinct values, sorted rows or the --join file kept (ceiling 1073741824). It counts what the call holds in its tables and rows by a stated formula, which is not resident memory: a join build that is still growing can be about twice that resident (measured 1.65 to 2.26 times on a spike). A join holds, for each row of the --join file, its record bytes plus 16 and, for each distinct key, its bytes plus 60\",\"default\":67108864},\"order-by\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"sort the rows by these keys, a comma-separated list: each key is a header name or #N, with - before it for descending and :int, :dec(S) or :float after it to compare by value: exact integers, exact decimals of at most S fractional digits, doubles (otherwise bytewise, so 10 sorts before 9 and an empty cell first). A later key breaks ties of an earlier one, and ties keep the order of the file. A cell that is not of the type refuses the query (value.not-integer, value.not-decimal, value.not-float and the like, context order-by). A name that starts with - or ends in :int, :dec(S) or :float is written \\\\-name or name\\\\:int. Rows are held up to --max-sort-rows, so keep it under with --where or --top. Not with --group or --agg. Examples: --order-by -bytes:int and --order-by status,-bytes:int and --order-by '-price:dec(2)'\"},\"report\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"read the whole table once and describe each column's cells instead of returning them: the only value is types. One row per column (--select picks the columns, --where keeps the rows): name, cells, empty, int, dec, float, not_finite, other, dec_max_scale, int_digits_max, widest, suggest, then the row and line of the first empty cell, of the first cell :int would refuse, :dec would refuse and :float would refuse, of the first other cell, that cell's first 64 bytes and whether it was longer. A cell is empty, an int (:int reads it), a dec (a point, no exponent, up to 18 digits: :dec(S) reads it with S the digits after the point), a float (an exponent, or more digits: :float reads it), not_finite (nan, inf) or other. suggest is the smallest declaration every non-empty cell satisfies, written as the suffix: :int, :dec(S) with S the most fractional digits seen, :float, or none (a cell that is not a number, nan or inf, or only empty cells). It is a suggestion and nothing applies it: no flag reads a column as a type unless you say so, and a float column of 0.1-shaped cells is :dec(1), the exact reading. --limit and --from page the columns, --max-bytes bounds a json page, and the counters take 248 bytes a column, counted in --max-state-bytes. Not with --group, --agg, --order-by, --sort or --top. Examples: --report types and --report types --select price,bytes --where 'status = 404'\"},\"max-sort-rows\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":20000000,\"description\":\"the most rows held to sort, one more is limit.too-many-sort-rows (ceiling 20000000)\",\"default\":1000000},\"threads\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":64,\"description\":\"how many threads read the file (1 to 64), for rows and groups only, on a file of at least --parallel-min-bytes of data: 1 is the sequential read and the answer of every other count is the same bytes. Standard input is always read by one thread (a pipe has no ranges to split), and the answer is still the same bytes\",\"default\":1},\"chunk-bytes\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":1073741824,\"description\":\"the size of the range one thread reads at a time (with --threads), at least 1\",\"default\":4194304},\"parallel-min-bytes\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":1073741824,\"description\":\"the smallest data (after the header) read by several threads, below it the read is sequential\",\"default\":1048576},\"max-rows\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":1000000000,\"description\":\"the most data rows read, past it the answer says truncated (ceiling 1000000000)\",\"default\":10000000},\"max-line-bytes\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":16777216,\"description\":\"the longest line read and the most a kept record may hold, a longer one is limit.line-too-long (ceiling 16777216)\",\"default\":1048576},\"format\":{\"enum\":[\"json\",\"text\",\"csv\",\"jsonl\"],\"description\":\"json for a program, one document on standard output, text for a person (the shape only, do not parse it), csv for rows or groups as csv (it needs --select, --where, --order-by, --group or --agg), jsonl for the same rows or groups as JSON lines: one object per line, keys the output columns in order (no header line, and two columns of one name are output.duplicate-column), every value a JSON string (a cell that is not UTF-8 is the object {\\\"b64\\\": \\\"...\\\"}, an empty cell is \\\"\\\") except the aggregates (count, sum, min, max, mean, distinct) and a group key with a type (--group NAME:int, NAME:dec(S) or NAME:float), which are JSON numbers written as csv writes them (a key is its value: 007 is 7). csv, jsonl and text are not a json document: standard output is only the csv or the lines, a refusal is one line on standard error as table: RULE: message with the exit status, and rows already written stand, so a non-zero exit means the output is a prefix\",\"default\":\"json\"},\"query\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"ask in a small subset of SQL, translated to the flags it stands for and run as they would be. select COLUMNS, or *, or count(*), sum(c), min(c), max(c), mean(c) or mean(c, N), count(distinct c), then optionally from 'FILE' or from stdin, where COND and COND, group by COLUMNS, order by KEY desc, limit N offset N. A COND is c = 'x', c != 5, c <> 5, c < 5 (also <=, > and >=), c contains 'x' or c in ('a', 'b'): only and, no or and no parentheses. A value is 'single quoted' text (a doubled quote is one quote) or a bare number. Text compares bytewise, so write cast(c as int) > 5 or c::int > 5 to compare numbers. The types are int, dec(N) and float. A name is bare (letters, digits and underscore, and not a keyword) or \\\"double quoted\\\" (spaces, commas, dots, keywords), and #3 is the third column. Keywords are not case sensitive and names are. A grouping selects its group by columns first, in the same order, then aggregates, and has at least one. A query is at most 4096 bytes. There are no aliases, expressions, joins, like, null or select distinct: each is refused as query.unsupported naming the feature and what to write instead, and query.syntax gives the byte offset and what was expected. Do not mix it with --select, --where, --group, --agg, --sort, --order-by, --rows or --report. --limit and --from stay flags unless the query has its own limit or offset, and --top stays a flag. A from in the query names the file, so then there is no FILE operand. Examples: --query \\\"select host, count(*) group by host order by count(*) desc\\\" and --query \\\"select host, bytes where status = 404 order by bytes::int desc limit 5\\\"\"},\"rows\":{\"type\":\"boolean\",\"description\":\"every row with all its columns, no condition: the form of select * with nothing else. It is the way to ask for a whole table as rows when there is no --select, --where or --group to say so, and it takes none of those, nor --order-by or --report. Example: --rows --format csv\"},\"explain\":{\"type\":\"boolean\",\"description\":\"print the plan the other flags (or --query) resolve to, as one JSON line, and do not run it: the source and how it is read, the columns with their positions, every condition, grouping, aggregate and order with its type, the page, the work the plan is (rows read, what is held, the bounds in force), the threads it would use and why, the rules the data could still refuse it with, and notes. A file is opened and its first 64 KiB read for the header, and nothing after the header is read. Standard input is not read at all, so its columns are not known. A plan the run would refuse before it read a record (a bad flag or query, a name the header lacks, a file that is missing, a header past --max-line-bytes) is refused with the error and the exit status of the run. Example: --explain --group status --agg sum:bytes sales.csv\"},\"input-format\":{\"enum\":[\"csv\",\"jsonl\",\"ndjson\"],\"description\":\"how the input is read: csv (the default, a header row names the columns), or jsonl, also spelled ndjson: JSON lines, one JSON object per line, whose columns are declared with --fields (never guessed from the name or the content of the input). A line is parsed as strict JSON (RFC 8259) and a line that is not an object is parse.jsonl-not-object. A number is the cell its exact text is, never through a double (the typed reads :int, :dec(S) and :float then read it as they read a csv cell), a string its decoded text, true and false the words. Standard input and a file give the same answer, and a file is read by --threads as a csv file is. Not with --delimiter, which then only sets the delimiter of csv output. Example: --input-format jsonl --fields id,status,user.name --where 'status:int >= 500' events.jsonl\",\"default\":\"csv\"},\"fields\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"with --input-format jsonl, the columns, by path, a comma-separated list: a path is keys separated by dots, and a number in the path is an index where the value is an array: id, user.name, items.0.sku. A backslash escapes the next byte, which must be a comma, a backslash, a # or a dot (a key with a dot is a\\\\.b). The column is called by the path as written, and --select, --where, --group, --agg, --order-by and #N use that name. Every declared path is looked up in every line, used or not, so declare what the plan reads. A value that is a string, a number, true or false is the cell. null, or a key or index that is not there, is the absent cell (see --absent). an object or an array is parse.jsonl-not-scalar. a key twice on the way to a declared path is parse.jsonl-duplicate-key. Required with --input-format jsonl (input.fields-required), and not with csv. Example: --fields id,status,user.name,tags.0\"},\"absent\":{\"enum\":[\"refuse\",\"empty\"],\"description\":\"with --input-format jsonl, what a declared path with no value is (null, a key or an index that is not there, a step through something that is not an object or an array): refuse (the default) is parse.jsonl-missing-field, naming the line, the row, the column and the reason, and empty is the empty cell (which the typed reads then refuse as an empty csv cell is refused: bytes != '' and bytes:int > 5). Not with csv\",\"default\":\"refuse\"},\"max-json-nodes\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":1048576,\"description\":\"with --input-format jsonl, the most JSON nodes a line may hold (a string, a number, a literal, a key, an array or an object is one), 1 to 1048576: the tape of a reader is three integers a node, made once. a line with more is limit.json-too-many-nodes. Not with csv\",\"default\":4096},\"discover\":{\"type\":\"boolean\",\"description\":\"with --input-format jsonl, instead of a plan, a bounded report of what the first lines hold, as data.discover: each path to depth 8 in order of first appearance, written as --fields takes it, with how many of the examined lines have a string, an int (a number with no point or exponent), a number, a bool, a null, an empty object, an array or nothing there, and the line it first appears on. lines_examined, complete (the input ended inside --discover-lines), paths_truncated (more than --max-fields paths), undeclarable (a path that --fields cannot say: an empty key) and arrays (the paths that hold an array, which is not expanded: declare tags.0 for the first). Nothing in it reaches a plan: copy the paths you want into --fields. Takes no plan flag, no --fields and no --format csv. Example: --input-format jsonl --discover events.jsonl\"},\"discover-lines\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":1000000,\"description\":\"with --discover, how many records to examine (1 to 1000000)\",\"default\":1000},\"max-fields\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":4096,\"description\":\"with --discover, how many paths to list (1 to 4096), past it paths_truncated is true\",\"default\":256},\"join\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"the build side of a join: a second CSV file beneath --root, resolved as FILE is (- is refused, since it is read whole before the first probe row). FILE (or standard input) is the probe side and is streamed, this file is held in memory and counted in --max-state-bytes. The answer is the inner join, rows only: for each probe row, in the probe order, one row for each row of this file whose key is equal to the probe row key, in the order of this file, the cells of the probe row and then the cells of the build row (a probe row with no match is not in it). It needs --on. --select, --where, --limit, --from and --format work on the joined row, and --group, --agg, --order-by, --sort, --top and --report do not yet, so pipe the join as csv into a second call. The first byte of output waits for the whole build, --threads reads on one core with the same bytes, and nothing is written to disk. Example: --join users.csv --on user=id events.csv\"},\"on\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"with --join, the key as PROBE=BUILD: a column of FILE and a column of the --join file, each a header name or #N, whose cells are equal when they are the same bytes (text: 007 is not 7, a quoted cell is the text inside its quotes, and an empty cell matches an empty cell, since there is no null). One pair and no type suffix in this version. A comma, a backslash, an equals sign, a colon or a leading # in a name is written with a backslash before it. A name that both files have is join.column-collision unless --join-prefix is given. Examples: --on user=id and --on #2=name\"},\"join-type\":{\"enum\":[\"inner\"],\"description\":\"with --join, which join: inner, the only one in this version, is each probe row once for every build row with its key\",\"default\":\"inner\"},\"join-prefix\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"with --join, text put before every column name of the --join file in the answer, so that files with a column of the same name can be joined (--join-prefix d_ makes the id column of the build file d_id, and it is named d_id in --select and --where). Without it a name that both files have is join.column-collision. Example: --join-prefix d_\"},\"max-join-rows\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0,\"maximum\":1000000000,\"description\":\"with --join, the most rows the join may produce before --where, 1 to 1000000000: one more is limit.too-many-join-rows, which says how many build rows the probe row key has (a key that repeats in the --join file multiplies the probe rows). A --limit that is met stops the probe early\",\"default\":10000000},\"file\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"the CSV file to read, a path relative to --root when given (absolute paths, .. and symbolic links are refused), or - or nothing for standard input (a file called - is ./-), and its first row is the header that --select, --where, --group, --agg and --order-by name columns by (with --input-format jsonl: the JSON-lines file, whose columns --fields declares) Give exactly one of `file` and `stdin`.\"},\"stdin\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The table itself, as text, instead of `file`: give exactly one of the two. It is read as `table -` reads standard input, so the answer is the one for a file with the same bytes (a refusal names the path `-`). UTF-8 text only (a NUL is allowed); at most 1048576 bytes by default (--max-input), else `mcp.input-too-large`. A `query` must not name a source with `from` when `stdin` is given: it reads this text. An empty string is an empty table.\"}},\"required\":[],\"additionalProperties\":false},\"annotations\":{\"readOnlyHint\":true,\"destructiveHint\":false,\"idempotentHint\":true,\"openWorldHint\":false}}]}";
}

pub fn count() -> [] int {
    return 1;
}

pub fn name(i: int) -> [] &static [byte] {
    if i == 0 {
        return "table";
    }
    return "";
}

pub fn flags(i: int) -> [] &static [byte] {
    if i == 0 {
        return "delimiter|--delimiter|text;select|--select|text;where|--where|text;group|--group|text;agg|--agg|text;sort|--sort|text;top|--top|nat;limit|--limit|nat;from|--from|nat;max-bytes|--max-bytes|nat;max-groups|--max-groups|nat;max-distinct|--max-distinct|nat;max-state-bytes|--max-state-bytes|nat;order-by|--order-by|text;report|--report|text;max-sort-rows|--max-sort-rows|nat;threads|--threads|nat;chunk-bytes|--chunk-bytes|nat;parallel-min-bytes|--parallel-min-bytes|nat;max-rows|--max-rows|nat;max-line-bytes|--max-line-bytes|nat;format|--format|text;query|--query|text;rows|--rows|bool;explain|--explain|bool;input-format|--input-format|text;fields|--fields|text;absent|--absent|text;max-json-nodes|--max-json-nodes|nat;discover|--discover|bool;discover-lines|--discover-lines|nat;max-fields|--max-fields|nat;join|--join|text;on|--on|text;join-type|--join-type|text;join-prefix|--join-prefix|text;max-join-rows|--max-join-rows|nat";
    }
    return "";
}

pub fn operands(i: int) -> [] &static [byte] {
    if i == 0 {
        return "file|0|1";
    }
    return "";
}

pub fn stdin(i: int) -> [] &static [byte] {
    if i == 0 {
        return "text";
    }
    return "";
}

pub fn output(i: int) -> [] &static [byte] {
    if i == 0 {
        return "document";
    }
    return "";
}

pub fn structured(i: int) -> [] &static [byte] {
    if i == 0 {
        return "format";
    }
    return "";
}

// The binary, beneath the directory the server's `Exec` is narrowed to.
pub fn path(i: int) -> [] &static [byte] {
    if i == 0 {
        return "/opt/cancho-table/bin/table";
    }
    return "";
}

pub fn default_timeout() -> [] int {
    return 30000;
}

pub fn default_output() -> [] int {
    return 2097152;
}

pub fn default_input() -> [] int {
    return 1048576;
}

pub fn longest_line() -> [] int {
    return 4194304;
}

pub fn most_errors() -> [] int {
    return 65536;
}

// What the server tells a caller whose call it had to end (the `hint` of the error).
pub fn hint_too_large() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "ask for less: `limit` caps the rows, `top` the groups (or the first rows of an order-by), `select` the columns, `where` the rows; do not raise `max-bytes` past the server's bound";
}

pub fn hint_timeout() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "ask for less work: add `where`, `limit` or `top`, name fewer columns with `select`, or read a large file with more `threads`; the deadline is the server's --timeout-ms";
}

pub fn hint_input() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "send less text: the header line and the rows that are needed, not the whole table; the bound is the server's --max-input";
}

// `mcp introspect`.
pub fn introspect() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "{\"tool\":\"mcp\",\"version\":\"0.1.0\",\"compiler\":\"e47d647353c4e35929f312cf07b0225c88b48b7c\",\"summary\":\"An MCP server (stdio, JSON-RPC 2.0) that serves the tool `table` under one root: its input schema is derived from the tool's `introspect`, a call is a fixed argv with no shell, the answer is the tool's stdout, stderr and exit code unchanged, bounded in size and in time.\",\"usage\":\"mcp --root DIR [--timeout-ms N] [--max-output N] [--max-input N] | mcp introspect\",\"output\":\"stdio\",\"protocol\":{\"transport\":\"stdio, one JSON-RPC 2.0 message per line\",\"version\":\"2025-06-18\",\"methods\":[\"initialize\",\"ping\",\"tools/list\",\"tools/call\"],\"notifications\":\"accepted and not answered\",\"max_line_bytes\":4194304},\"serves\":{\"tool\":\"table\",\"version\":\"0.3.0\",\"compiler\":\"e47d647353c4e35929f312cf07b0225c88b48b7c\",\"bin\":\"/opt/cancho-table/bin\"},\"tools\":[\"table\"],\"flags\":[{\"name\":\"--root\",\"kind\":\"path\",\"role\":\"root\",\"default\":null,\"help\":\"the directory the tool serves: every FILE is resolved beneath it by the tool, which refuses absolute paths, `..` and links; required, absolute, given to the tool first and once\"},{\"name\":\"--timeout-ms\",\"kind\":\"nat\",\"role\":\"none\",\"default\":30000,\"help\":\"the deadline of one call: the tool is killed past it and the call ends as mcp.timeout\"},{\"name\":\"--max-output\",\"kind\":\"nat\",\"role\":\"none\",\"default\":2097152,\"help\":\"the most standard output of one call, in bytes: the tool is killed past it and the call ends as mcp.output-too-large\"},{\"name\":\"--max-input\",\"kind\":\"nat\",\"role\":\"none\",\"default\":1048576,\"help\":\"the most text of one call's `stdin` property, in decoded bytes: past it the call is refused before the tool starts, as mcp.input-too-large\"}],\"limits\":[{\"name\":\"timeout-ms\",\"default\":30000},{\"name\":\"max-output\",\"default\":2097152},{\"name\":\"max-input\",\"default\":1048576,\"note\":\"the `stdin` text of a call, in decoded bytes; the request line (below) bounds it from above\"},{\"name\":\"stderr\",\"default\":65536,\"note\":\"fixed: the tool's standard error beyond it ends the call as mcp.output-too-large\"},{\"name\":\"line\",\"default\":4194304,\"note\":\"fixed: a request line beyond it is refused as mcp.line-too-long\"}],\"rules\":[{\"rule\":\"mcp.parse-error\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32700\",\"summary\":\"the line is not one JSON value\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.invalid-request\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32600\",\"summary\":\"not a JSON-RPC 2.0 request\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.line-too-long\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32600\",\"summary\":\"a request line longer than max_line_bytes\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.unknown-method\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32601\",\"summary\":\"a method other than initialize, ping, tools/list, tools/call\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.unknown-tool\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32602\",\"summary\":\"a tool name other than table\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.arguments-not-object\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32602\",\"summary\":\"`arguments` is not an object\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.unknown-argument\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32602\",\"summary\":\"a property the input schema does not have (data lists the real ones)\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.wrong-type\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32602\",\"summary\":\"a property of the wrong type; every fault is listed in data.problems\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.nul-in-argument\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32602\",\"summary\":\"a NUL byte in a string, which would arrive as two arguments\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.input-conflict\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32602\",\"summary\":\"both `file` and `stdin` given: the call has one input (data.properties names them)\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.input-missing\",\"answer\":\"jsonrpc -32602\",\"summary\":\"neither `file` nor `stdin` given: the call has no input (data.properties names them)\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.input-too-large\",\"answer\":\"isError result\",\"summary\":\"the `stdin` text passed --max-input; send less text: the header line and the rows that are needed, not the whole table; the bound is the server's --max-input\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.timeout\",\"answer\":\"isError result\",\"summary\":\"the call ran past --timeout-ms; ask for less work: add `where`, `limit` or `top`, name fewer columns with `select`, or read a large file with more `threads`; the deadline is the server's --timeout-ms\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.output-too-large\",\"answer\":\"isError result\",\"summary\":\"the output passed --max-output (or the error stream passed its bound); ask for less: `limit` caps the rows, `top` the groups (or the first rows of an order-by), `select` the columns, `where` the rows; do not raise `max-bytes` past the server's bound\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.signaled\",\"answer\":\"isError result\",\"summary\":\"the tool was ended by a signal\"},{\"rule\":\"mcp.spawn\",\"answer\":\"isError result\",\"summary\":\"the tool could not be started or watched\"}],\"confinement\":{\"root\":\"every path argument is the tool's, resolved by it beneath --root; the server passes --root first and once, flag values as one `--name=value` argument and operands after `--`, with no shell\",\"exec\":\"the binary of the directory its Exec is narrowed to (baked at build time), by one fixed name\"},\"authority\":{\"bounded\":true,\"unbounded_by\":[],\"effects\":[\"args\",\"child_signal\",\"clock\",\"err_write\",\"exec\",\"heap\",\"io_read\",\"io_write\",\"pipe_read\",\"pipe_write\",\"poll\"],\"labels\":[{\"name\":\"args\",\"argument\":null,\"bounded\":true},{\"name\":\"child_signal\",\"argument\":null,\"bounded\":true},{\"name\":\"clock\",\"argument\":null,\"bounded\":true},{\"name\":\"err_write\",\"argument\":null,\"bounded\":true},{\"name\":\"exec\",\"argument\":\"/opt/cancho-table/bin\",\"bounded\":true},{\"name\":\"heap\",\"argument\":null,\"bounded\":true},{\"name\":\"io_read\",\"argument\":null,\"bounded\":true},{\"name\":\"io_write\",\"argument\":null,\"bounded\":true},{\"name\":\"pipe_read\",\"argument\":null,\"bounded\":true},{\"name\":\"pipe_write\",\"argument\":null,\"bounded